<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ballana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ballana is an archaeological site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a> on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, now under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>. Excavations by Walter Emery in the 1930s uncovered large burial mounds of rulers of the 4th to 6th centuries CE, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> crowns, weapons and sacrificed horses and servants. The site gave its name to the Ballana culture, associated with the kingdom of Nobadia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ballana.jpg" alt="Ballana" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 10647023@N04 (Flickr) (Mr. John Campana) · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_post-Meroitic_era_Nubian_royal_crown_from_Ballana_Tomb_118_by_John_Campana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
